// Database Backup Runs (issue #281, epic #254)
// =============================================================================
//
// One row per attempt to dump this deployment's PostgreSQL database into
// object storage — and, from Phase 7 onwards, per attempt to restore FROM one.
// `DatabaseBackupRunnerService` (`src/db-backup/db-backup-runner.service.ts`)
// is the only writer of the backup half; the columns in the restore-audit
// group are declared here now and written by #285.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ### Why this WAS not a queue job, and why it now is (issue #351, epic #345)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ⚠ THIS SECTION USED TO SAY "DO NOT MIGRATE THIS ONTO THE QUEUE". It has
// been migrated, deliberately, and the history is kept because the three
// objections were correct and a reader needs to know WHERE EACH IS NOW
// ANSWERED rather than assume they were waved away.
//
//   1. `jobs.stuckThresholdMinutes` DEFAULTS TO 30 MINUTES. A dump of a real
//      production database routinely runs longer than that. The lease reaper
//      (`JobStuckResetTask`) would see a `running` job whose lease had
//      expired, conclude its executor had died, and RESET IT TO `pending` —
//      while `pg_dump` was still streaming. The next claim starts a SECOND
//      `pg_dump`, writing to the SAME derived storage key as the first, and
//      two processes interleave their output into one object.
//      → ANSWERED BY #346's PER-TYPE PROFILES. `db.backup.run` declares
//      `maxRuntimeMs: 6h`, and the claim's lease is DERIVED from that ceiling
//      (`resolveJobLeaseMs`), never declared beside it — so a lease shorter
//      than the permitted runtime is unrepresentable rather than merely
//      avoided.
//   2. THE IN-PROCESS WORKER HAS NO LEASE-RENEWAL PATH. `JobWorker` claimed
//      with a lease and never extended it, so the reaper's verdict above was
//      not a rare race but unconditional for any long job.
//      → ANSWERED BY #347. The worker renews on a ticker (lease ÷ 3) for
//      exactly as long as `process()` runs.
//   3. A JOB HAS `attempts` AND A RETRY BUDGET. Automatically re-running a
//      failed multi-gigabyte dump is exactly the wrong behaviour: it burns
//      hours of I/O on a database that is probably already unwell, and the
//      correct retry for a backup is "the next scheduled one", tomorrow.
//      → ANSWERED BY `maxAttempts: 1` on the same profile. The policy did not
//      change; what changed is that `JobStuckService`'s give-up phase now
//      ENFORCES it, instead of it holding by the accident of there being no
//      queue to disagree.
//
// This table still has its OWN heartbeat (`lastHeartbeatAt`, written every
// ~20s with live `bytesWritten`), its OWN staleness policy
// (`databaseBackup.runStaleMinutes`) and its OWN terminal states, and all
// three are still load-bearing — but their jobs have narrowed. The heartbeat
// is now PROGRESS REPORTING for the UI (the lease is the liveness signal),
// and the stale sweep is the backstop for the one path that still has no job:
// the `pre_restore` safety dump, which `DatabaseRestoreService` takes through
// `startBackup` because a restore must not wait on a worker slot.
//
// ⚠ THE TWO ROWS ARE NOT REDUNDANT. The `jobs` row answers who asked, when,
// which executor, which attempt and what error; this row answers what was
// PRODUCED — key, bucket, bytes, checksum, `verifiedAt`, the db/app/migration
// audit and the restore audit. They also have independent lifetimes: a job is
// purged by `job.history.purge` on a retention schedule that has nothing to do
// with how long the archive is kept, which is exactly why `jobId` is
// `onDelete: SetNull` and not `Cascade`.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SINGLE-ACTIVE-RUN GUARD IS A PARTIAL UNIQUE INDEX, NOT APPLICATION CODE
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `database_backup_runs_active_uniq_idx` (hand-written in
// `prisma/migrations/20260907120000_add_database_backup_runs/migration.sql`,
// TIGHTENED by
// `prisma/migrations/<add_backup_run_job_link>/migration.sql`, epic #345,
// issue #351 — see that migration's own header for why) is a UNIQUE index on
// the CONSTANT EXPRESSION `(true)` restricted to `status IN
// ('pending','running')`. It is what makes "at most one backup at a time"
// true ACROSS REPLICAS.
//
// REJECTED: a `findFirst({ where: { status: 'running' } })` before the insert.
// It is a check-then-act race, and it is racy exactly when it matters — a
// scheduled tick on replica A and an admin's manual click on replica B, in
// the same second. Both would see no active run, both would insert, and two
// `pg_dump` processes would stream into two objects while the settings say
// one backup a night. Only the database can make "is one already active"
// atomic with the insert that would violate it.
//
// The loser's Prisma `P2002` becomes a typed `DatabaseBackupAlreadyRunningError`
// carrying the winner's run id, which #282/#283 turn into a 409.
//
// ⚠ PRISMA CANNOT EXPRESS THIS INDEX — INTENTIONAL SCHEMA DRIFT. The schema
// language has no syntax for a partial index OR an expression index, exactly
// as recorded above the `Job` model for `jobs_active_dedup_uniq_idx` and its
// two siblings. So `prisma migrate dev`/`diff` will not regenerate it and, if
// anyone diffs this schema against a live database, will want to DROP it.
// That drift is deliberate and permanent: do not "fix" it, and do not try to
// force it into `@@unique` syntax that does not exist. The migration file
// carries the same warning next to the statement itself.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THE INDEX ADMITS, PRECISELY, AND WHY THAT CHANGED. The ORIGINAL index
// keyed on the `status` COLUMN, filtered to two values — that admits at most
// one `pending` row AND at most one `running` row AT THE SAME TIME, i.e. TWO
// active runs, not one. That was harmless only as long as
// `DatabaseBackupRunnerService` inserted its claim directly as `running` and
// never wrote `pending`. #351 makes the backup a queue job: `POST
// /api/admin/db-backup/runs` now enqueues the job and creates the run row in
// `pending` BEFORE any worker has claimed it (so the endpoint can return a run
// id immediately and `GET /runs/{id}` keeps working), and the handler flips it
// to `running` once claimed. The moment `pending` rows are real, the
// column-keyed index stops meaning "at most one active run" and starts
// meaning "at most two". Keying on `(true)` instead makes every active row —
// whichever of the two statuses it holds — index to the SAME key, so at most
// one can exist across both statuses COMBINED. This is the exact tightening
// this comment always said a future path needing both states populated at
// once would require ("tighten this index to a constant expression, never
// move the guard into a service") — it has now happened.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THE RESTORE AUDIT LIVES ON THE BACKUP'S OWN ROW
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `restoreStatus`, `restoreError`, `restoredAt`, `restoredById`,
// `restoreScratchDb`, `restoreOldDb`, `swappedAt` and `preRestoreBackupId` are
// all nullable and all belong to Phase 7. They are columns on THIS table
// rather than a `database_restore_runs` table because A RESTORE IS ALWAYS
// DEFINED IN TERMS OF EXACTLY ONE BACKUP: there is no restore without an
// archive to restore, no archive is restored twice concurrently, and every
// question anyone asks about a restore ("which dump was this?", "how big was
// it?", "what migration was the schema on?") is answered by the backup's own
// columns. A separate table would buy a join on a strict 1:1 relationship and
// would let the two rows disagree about which archive was replayed.
//
// The cost is honest: a backup restored twice (a restore that failed, then
// retried) overwrites the first attempt's audit fields. That is the correct
// trade — the interesting record is the state of the LAST restore, and the
// full history of attempts is in `audit_events`.
//
// `preRestoreBackupId` is a SELF-FK: before a restore swaps a database away,
// #285 takes a `pre_restore` backup, and this column points the restore at the
// safety net it took. `onDelete: SetNull`, so pruning that safety backup by
// retention never deletes the restore record that referenced it.
